## Task

Implement a support ticket classifier in the .NET 10 Web API project at `/tmp/sv-c6fe232bacfe4f91964e13739c4aefe8/TicketClassifier`.

## Current state

- `TicketClassifier.csproj`: targets `net10.0`, has `AddControllers` / `MapControllers` in Program.cs but no controllers yet.
- `Data/tickets.csv`: Has 6 rows with columns Subject, Description, Priority, Category (Billing/Technical/General).
- Program.cs is minimal (5 lines).

## Requirements

Use ML.NET (Microsoft.ML) for multi-class classification. Follow ALL of these guardrails from the technology-selection skill:

1. **Reproducibility**: `new MLContext(seed: 42)`
2. **Data splitting**: `TrainTestSplit` with `testFraction: 0.2`
3. **Metrics logging**: Log multi-class evaluation metrics (MicroAccuracy, MacroAccuracy, LogLoss)
4. **PredictionEnginePool**: Use `PredictionEnginePool<TicketInput, TicketPrediction>` in ASP.NET Core (never a singleton PredictionEngine)
5. **DI registration**: All services registered via DI

## Implementation plan

### 1. Expand `Data/tickets.csv`
The existing 6 rows are too few for meaningful ML. Expand to ~30 rows with realistic support ticket data across all 3 categories (Billing, Technical, General), with varied priorities 1-3. Keep existing rows, add new ones.

### 2. Add NuGet packages to `TicketClassifier.csproj`
```xml
<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.ML" Version="4.*" />
<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.ML" Version="4.*" />
```
(`Microsoft.Extensions.ML` provides `PredictionEnginePool` integration with ASP.NET Core DI)

### 3. Create `Models/TicketInput.cs`
```csharp
using Microsoft.ML.Data;

namespace TicketClassifier.Models;

public class TicketInput
{
    [LoadColumn(0)]
    public string Subject { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [LoadColumn(1)]
    public string Description { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [LoadColumn(2)]
    public float Priority { get; set; }

    [LoadColumn(3)]
    public string Category { get; set; } = string.Empty;
}
```

### 4. Create `Models/TicketPrediction.cs`
```csharp
using Microsoft.ML.Data;

namespace TicketClassifier.Models;

public class TicketPrediction
{
    [ColumnName("PredictedLabel")]
    public string PredictedCategory { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    public float[] Score { get; set; } = [];
}
```

### 5. Create `Services/ModelTrainerService.cs`
This service:
- Loads the CSV
- Builds an ML pipeline: featurize text (Subject, Description), concatenate with Priority, map label to key, use `SdcaMaximumEntropy` trainer, map key back to label
- Splits data (train/test)
- Trains the model
- Evaluates and logs metrics (MicroAccuracy, MacroAccuracy, LogLoss)
- Saves the model to `Data/model.zip`

```csharp
using Microsoft.ML;
using TicketClassifier.Models;

namespace TicketClassifier.Services;

public class ModelTrainerService
{
    private readonly ILogger<ModelTrainerService> _logger;

    public ModelTrainerService(ILogger<ModelTrainerService> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public string TrainAndSaveModel()
    {
        var mlContext = new MLContext(seed: 42);

        var dataPath = Path.Combine(AppContext.BaseDirectory, "Data", "tickets.csv");
        var modelPath = Path.Combine(AppContext.BaseDirectory, "Data", "model.zip");

        IDataView data = mlContext.Data.LoadFromTextFile<TicketInput>(
            dataPath, hasHeader: true, separatorChar: ',', allowQuoting: true);

        var split = mlContext.Data.TrainTestSplit(data, testFraction: 0.2, seed: 42);

        var pipeline = mlContext.Transforms.Conversion.MapValueToKey("Label", nameof(TicketInput.Category))
            .Append(mlContext.Transforms.Text.FeaturizeText("SubjectFeaturized", nameof(TicketInput.Subject)))
            .Append(mlContext.Transforms.Text.FeaturizeText("DescriptionFeaturized", nameof(TicketInput.Description)))
            .Append(mlContext.Transforms.Concatenate("Features", "SubjectFeaturized", "DescriptionFeaturized", nameof(TicketInput.Priority)))
            .Append(mlContext.MulticlassClassification.Trainers.SdcaMaximumEntropy("Label", "Features"))
            .Append(mlContext.Transforms.Conversion.MapKeyToValue("PredictedLabel"));

        var model = pipeline.Fit(split.TrainSet);

        var predictions = model.Transform(split.TestSet);
        var metrics = mlContext.MulticlassClassification.Evaluate(predictions);

        _logger.LogInformation("Model evaluation metrics:");
        _logger.LogInformation("  MicroAccuracy: {MicroAccuracy:F4}", metrics.MicroAccuracy);
        _logger.LogInformation("  MacroAccuracy: {MacroAccuracy:F4}", metrics.MacroAccuracy);
        _logger.LogInformation("  LogLoss:       {LogLoss:F4}", metrics.LogLoss);
        _logger.LogInformation("  LogLossReduction: {LogLossReduction:F4}", metrics.LogLossReduction);

        mlContext.Model.Save(model, split.TrainSet.Schema, modelPath);
        _logger.LogInformation("Model saved to {ModelPath}", modelPath);

        return modelPath;
    }
}
```

### 6. Create `Controllers/TicketController.cs`

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using Microsoft.Extensions.ML;
using TicketClassifier.Models;

namespace TicketClassifier.Controllers;

[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class TicketController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly PredictionEnginePool<TicketInput, TicketPrediction> _predictionPool;

    public TicketController(PredictionEnginePool<TicketInput, TicketPrediction> predictionPool)
    {
        _predictionPool = predictionPool;
    }

    [HttpPost("classify")]
    public ActionResult<TicketClassificationResult> Classify([FromBody] TicketRequest request)
    {
        var input = new TicketInput
        {
            Subject = request.Subject,
            Description = request.Description,
            Priority = request.Priority
        };

        var prediction = _predictionPool.Predict(input);

        return Ok(new TicketClassificationResult
        {
            PredictedCategory = prediction.PredictedCategory,
            Confidence = prediction.Score.Max(),
            Scores = new Dictionary<string, float>
            {
                ["Billing"] = prediction.Score.ElementAtOrDefault(0),
                ["General"] = prediction.Score.ElementAtOrDefault(1),
                ["Technical"] = prediction.Score.ElementAtOrDefault(2)
            }
        });
    }
}

public class TicketRequest
{
    public string Subject { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public string Description { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public float Priority { get; set; }
}

public class TicketClassificationResult
{
    public string PredictedCategory { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public float Confidence { get; set; }
    public Dictionary<string, float> Scores { get; set; } = new();
}
```

### 7. Update `Program.cs`
```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.ML;
using TicketClassifier.Models;
using TicketClassifier.Services;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.AddControllers();
builder.Services.AddSingleton<ModelTrainerService>();

// Train model on startup
var sp = builder.Services.BuildServiceProvider();
var trainer = sp.GetRequiredService<ModelTrainerService>();
var modelPath = trainer.TrainAndSaveModel();

builder.Services.AddPredictionEnginePool<TicketInput, TicketPrediction>()
    .FromFile(modelPath);

var app = builder.Build();
app.MapControllers();
app.Run();
```

Wait, using `BuildServiceProvider()` in the middle is an anti-pattern. Better approach: use a hosted service or just train inline before building. Let me revise:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.ML;
using TicketClassifier.Models;
using TicketClassifier.Services;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.AddControllers();

// Train model at startup
var trainer = new ModelTrainerService(
    LoggerFactory.Create(b => b.AddConsole()).CreateLogger<ModelTrainerService>());
var modelPath = trainer.TrainAndSaveModel();

builder.Services.AddPredictionEnginePool<TicketInput, TicketPrediction>()
    .FromFile(modelPath);

var app = builder.Build();
app.MapControllers();
app.Run();
```

This is simpler but still a bit awkward. Actually the cleaner approach is to just do the training in Program.cs with explicit ML code or use a static method. Let me keep the service but instantiate it simply.

### 8. Update .csproj to copy Data folder to output

Add:
```xml
<ItemGroup>
  <Content Include="Data\**" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
</ItemGroup>
```

### 9. Build and verify

Run `dotnet build` to verify compilation. Run the app briefly to verify model training metrics appear in console and the endpoint works.

## Important notes
- Create directories `Models`, `Services`, `Controllers` under `/tmp/sv-c6fe232bacfe4f91964e13739c4aefe8/TicketClassifier/`
- The score array indices map to the alphabetical order of the label values that ML.NET discovers during training. Since we don't know the exact order at compile time, the controller should discover the label mapping dynamically. However for simplicity with 3 known categories, we can note this caveat.
- Actually, better approach for the Scores dictionary: we should get the slot names from the model schema rather than hardcoding. But for this demo keeping it simple is fine — just add a comment noting the indices depend on training order.

Please implement all of this. After creating the files:
1. Run `dotnet restore` and `dotnet build` in the project directory
2. If there are compilation errors, fix them
3. Start the app briefly with `dotnet run` and test the endpoint with curl, then stop it
4. Make sure everything works end-to-end

